(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-physiology-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture's hypothalamic control diagram, which posterior pituitary target organ list is correct for the ADH pathway?","choices":["Thyroid gland","Adrenal cortex","Kidneys","Gonads"],"answer":2,"exp":"ADH's target organ shown in the hypothalamic control diagram is the kidneys. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is aldosterone's primary site of action?","choices":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Loop of Henle","Renal collecting duct","Bowman's capsule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Aldosterone acts primarily at the renal collecting duct. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What role does progesterone play in preparing for implantation and early pregnancy nutrition?","choices":["It has essentially no meaningful physiological role at all until the onset of parturition itself.","It solely and directly regulates the developing fetal heart rate throughout gestation.","It only affects maternal mood and emotional state, with no direct uterine role.","It prepares the uterine lining for implantation, which then provides nutrients to the conceptus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Progesterone prepares the uterine lining for implantation, and that lining then provides nutrients to the conceptus (embryo plus adjacent membranes). — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Per the lecture's innervation summary table, the hypogastric nerve (T11–L2/L1–L2) functions primarily in:","choices":["Detrusor contraction","External sphincter control","Sensory stretch detection only","Storage"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hypogastric nerve's listed function is storage. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Using the lecture's shorthand summary, PTH is described as doing what to calcium and phosphate?","choices":["Tosses calcium, preserves phosphate","Preserves calcium, tosses phosphate (secretion)","Tosses both calcium and phosphate","Preserves both calcium and phosphate"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the lecture's mnemonic, PTH 'preserves calcium, tosses phosphate' — i.e., promotes calcium reabsorption while promoting phosphate excretion (secretion). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In smooth muscle, the structures that anchor thin filaments and are considered analogous to the Z discs of striated muscle are the:","choices":["Gap junctions","Terminal cisternae","Intermediate filaments","Dense bodies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: dense bodies are the membrane-associated dense areas analogous to Z discs. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"},{"q":"Thyrotropes secrete TSH, whose function is to:","choices":["Stimulate secretion of glucocorticoids from the adrenal cortex","Stimulate milk production","Stimulate spermatogenesis in the testis","Stimulate production of thyroid hormone by follicular cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"TSH stimulates production of thyroid hormone by the thyroid's follicular cells. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Aldosterone is transported in the blood bound to CBG and which other class of plasma proteins?","choices":["Hemoglobin molecules","Albumin and prealbumin","Immunoglobulins","Fibrinogen molecules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Aldosterone is transported bound to corticosteroid binding globulin (CBG) as well as other plasma proteins including albumin and prealbumin. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where does fertilization of the ovum typically occur?","choices":["In the ovary itself, before ovulation occurs","In the lower portion of the cervical canal","In the upper cavity of the uterus","In the ampullae of the fallopian tubes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fertilization occurs in the ampullae of the fallopian tubes. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Renal autoregulation keeps GFR and renal blood flow relatively constant across approximately what range of mean arterial pressure?","choices":["80–180 mmHg","180–250 mmHg","40–80 mmHg","There is no defined range — autoregulation works at all pressures"],"answer":0,"exp":"Renal autoregulation maintains a relatively constant GFR and renal blood flow across arterial pressures of approximately 80–180 mmHg. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Besides reduced perfusion pressure and reduced Na+ delivery, what third trigger activates renin release via the JGA?","choices":["Sympathetic stimulation via β1 adrenoreceptors","Increased plasma calcium","Parasympathetic stimulation via muscarinic receptors","Increased plasma glucose"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation of the JGA via β1 adrenoreceptors is the third listed trigger for renin release, alongside reduced perfusion pressure and reduced Na+ delivery. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the function of osteocalcin, and which vitamin assists it?","choices":["It degrades collagen fibers; it is assisted by vitamin C","It bonds hydroxyapatite to collagen; it is assisted by vitamin K","It resorbs bone matrix; it is assisted by vitamin D","It transports calcium in the plasma; it is assisted by vitamin A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: osteocalcin bonds hydroxyapatite to collagen, and vitamin K helps it do this. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 51"},{"q":"The growth hormone receptor is best classified as which type of receptor?","choices":["An enzyme-linked receptor","A nuclear steroid receptor","A G-protein-coupled receptor exclusively","An ion channel receptor"],"answer":0,"exp":"The growth hormone receptor is enzyme-linked. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Cortisol mobilizes fatty acids for energy partly by:","choices":["Blocking fatty acid oxidation entirely, forcing exclusive reliance on glucose","Increasing pancreatic insulin secretion directly in order to mobilize stored fat","Decreasing glucose transport into fat cells, shifting fuel use from glucose to fat","Increasing glucose transport into fat cells, favoring fat storage over use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cortisol decreases glucose transport into fat cells, which helps shift fuel utilization from glucose to fat, alongside enhancing fatty acid oxidation. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Cervical stretching during labor stimulates oxytocin secretion from which structure?","choices":["The ovary itself, via local paracrine hormonal signaling","Supraoptic nuclei of the hypothalamus (via posterior pituitary release)","The anterior pituitary, via releasing-hormone stimulation","The adrenal medulla, via direct sympathetic nervous activation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cervical stretching stimulates oxytocin secretion, with oxytocin originating from the supraoptic nuclei of the hypothalamus and released via the posterior pituitary. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Under normal conditions, how do higher brain centers typically influence the spinal micturition reflex?","choices":["They only ever facilitate and progressively strengthen the reflex, and are never capable of inhibiting it.","They permanently and completely abolish the underlying spinal micturition reflex once puberty has been reached.","They have no influence at all on the reflex, which operates as a purely isolated, self-contained spinal circuit.","They keep the reflex partially inhibited except when urination is desired, and can prevent it via tonic contraction of the external sphincter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Higher centers normally keep the micturition reflex partially inhibited except when urination is desired, and can prevent micturition — even if the reflex occurs — through tonic contraction of the external sphincter. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus is composed of which three structures?","choices":["Proximal tubule cells, loop of Henle cells, and collecting duct cells","Afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, and Bowman's capsule structures","Principal cells, intercalated cells, and glomerular podocytes","Macula densa, granular (JG) cells, and extraglomerular mesangial cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"The juxtaglomerular apparatus is composed of the macula densa, granular (JG) cells, and extraglomerular mesangial cells. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Unitary (visceral) smooth muscle, such as that of the gut and blood vessels, behaves as a functional syncytium mainly because:","choices":["Each cell is individually controlled by its own dedicated motor neuron","Its cells are electrically coupled by gap junctions and contract in unison","Its cells are physically fused into a single giant multinucleated fiber","Its cells are densely innervated and contract only when stimulated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: unitary/visceral muscle is a sheet of electrically coupled cells that acts together, often spontaneously. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 40"},{"q":"Approximately how low is typical plasma hormone concentration, as emphasized in this lecture?","choices":["Picogram (10⁻¹² g/mL) range","Microgram (10⁻⁶ g/mL) range","Nanogram (10⁻⁹ g/mL) range","Milligram (10⁻³ g/mL) range"],"answer":0,"exp":"Plasma hormone concentration is emphasized as extremely low — on the order of a picogram (0.000000000001 g/mL blood). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What happens to aldosterone secretion when extracellular fluid (ECF) volume increases?","choices":["Aldosterone secretion is unaffected by ECF volume","Aldosterone secretion decreases","Aldosterone secretion increases","Aldosterone secretion becomes erratic and unpredictable"],"answer":1,"exp":"Increased ECF volume decreases aldosterone secretion. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Puberty's gradual onset is marked by GnRH beginning around which age?","choices":["The 18th year of life","The 8th year of life","The 30th year of life","The moment of birth"],"answer":1,"exp":"GnRH gradually starts around the 8th year of life, with puberty onset typically occurring between ages 11–16. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Creatinine, used to estimate GFR via clearance, is produced by which tissue?","choices":["Skeletal muscle","The pancreas","Adipose tissue","Hepatic tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Creatinine is produced by skeletal muscle, is freely filtered, and is only minimally secreted, making its clearance a practical (if slightly overestimating) proxy for true GFR. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"If NaCl delivery to the macula densa increases, what happens to renin release?","choices":["Renin release increases sharply","Renin is converted immediately to aldosterone","Renin release is suppressed","Renin release is unaffected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Increased NaCl delivery to the macula densa suppresses renin release (the inverse of the low-NaCl-delivery response, which increases renin). — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Muscle lengthening that occurs with normal growth produces which of the following?","choices":["Increased maximum force of contraction with no change in velocity","Formation of new muscle fibers","Splitting of myofibrils with added actin and myosin contractile protein","Increased shortening capacity and maximum contraction velocity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: lengthening increases shortening capacity and maximum velocity of contraction. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 37"},{"q":"Regulatory hormones from the hypothalamus reach the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) via which route?","choices":["Direct nerve fiber connections only","Diffusion through cerebrospinal fluid","The hypophyseal portal system","Systemic arterial circulation only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Regulatory hormones are released into the hypophyseal portal system for delivery specifically to the anterior lobe of the pituitary. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Adrenal steroids are cleared via which two general routes described in this lecture?","choices":["Only through direct excretion via eccrine sweat gland secretion","Through the liver into bile/feces and processing involving the kidney","Only through direct breakdown by skeletal muscle metabolism","Only through direct diffusion into the atmosphere via the lungs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Adrenal steroid clearance involves the liver (bile/feces route, with glucuronic acid conjugation) and the kidney, alongside plasma handling. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is secreted from which structure, and is detectable as early as how many days postovulation?","choices":["Corpus luteum tissue; detectable at around 30 days postovulation","Fetal adrenal cortex tissue; detectable at around 20 weeks postovulation","Syncytial trophoblasts; detectable as early as 8 days postovulation","Maternal pituitary gland; detectable at around 1 day postovulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"hCG is secreted from syncytial trophoblasts and is detectable as early as 8 days postovulation, peaking at 10–12 weeks. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"The micturition reflex is best defined as:","choices":["A reflex that can only ever inhibit bladder emptying and can never facilitate or enhance it in any way","A purely voluntary skeletal muscle contraction with no spinal cord involvement","A reflex confined entirely to the cerebral cortex, with no spinal component at all","An autonomic spinal cord reflex that empties the bladder, or if it fails, causes a conscious desire to urinate"],"answer":3,"exp":"The micturition reflex is a nervous, autonomic spinal cord reflex that empties the bladder; if it fails to do so, it causes a conscious desire to urinate instead. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What ultimately establishes the corticopapillary/corticomedullary osmotic gradient?","choices":["The passive diffusion of plasma proteins directly into the surrounding medullary interstitium","A single, isolated filtration event that occurs just once, very early in the glomerulus itself","The repeated single-effect and fluid-displacement cycle concentrating fluid toward the medulla and diluting it toward the cortex","The active, repeated secretion of glucose molecules directly into the collecting duct lumen itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"The repeated cycling of the single effect and fluid displacement — concentrating fluid as it descends and diluting it as it ascends — establishes the corticopapillary/corticomedullary osmotic gradient. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes an isometric contraction?","choices":["The muscle lengthens as it is overpowered by a light load","The muscle shortens at its maximum velocity","The muscle develops force without changing its length","The muscle shortens while lifting a constant load"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: in an isometric contraction the muscle generates force while its length stays fixed. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the chemical structure of growth hormone, as described in this lecture?","choices":["A gas-phase signaling molecule","A steroid derived from cholesterol","A tyrosine derivative bound to thyroxine-binding globulin","A protein hormone of 191 amino acids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Growth hormone is a protein hormone composed of 191 amino acids. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"The 75% category of adrenal cortical hormones (cortisol, corticosterone, androgens, estrogens) is primarily controlled by:","choices":["Angiotensin II","ACTH","Aldosterone itself","Renin"],"answer":1,"exp":"This 75% category, dominated by cortisol, is controlled by ACTH. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is hCG's major function regarding the corpus luteum in early pregnancy?","choices":["It prevents involution of the corpus luteum and stimulates its estrogen/progesterone production","It has essentially no functional relationship to the corpus luteum at any point.","It causes immediate and rapid involution of the corpus luteum within days","It gradually converts the corpus luteum directly into functioning placental tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"hCG prevents involution of the corpus luteum (increasing its function about 2×) and stimulates estrogen/progesterone secretion from it, until the corpus luteum slowly involutes after 12 weeks as hCG declines. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the effect of mild-to-moderate efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR?","choices":["Neither glomerular hydrostatic pressure nor GFR changes appreciably","Both glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR decrease substantially","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure increases and GFR increases slightly","Glomerular hydrostatic pressure decreases while GFR increases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mild-to-moderate efferent arteriolar constriction increases glomerular hydrostatic pressure, which increases GFR slightly. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Type B intercalated cells are responsible for:","choices":["HCO3- secretion","Na+ reabsorption via ENaC","ADH-dependent water reabsorption","H+ secretion and HCO3- reabsorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Type B intercalated cells secrete HCO3-, the opposite function from Type A cells. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Small motor units, such as those of the larynx and extraocular muscles, are characterized by which of the following?","choices":["As few as 10 fibers per unit and precise control","Slow-reacting fibers that fatigue readily","Coarse control suited for powerful movements","As many as 1000 fibers per unit and coarse control"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: small motor units may have as few as 10 fibers and provide precise, rapid control. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 35"},{"q":"Per the lecture's histologic staining classification, which anterior pituitary cell types are acidophilic?","choices":["Thyrotropes and gonadotropes","Corticotropes and thyrotropes","Somatotropes and mammotropes","Gonadotropes and corticotropes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Somatotropes and mammotropes (lactotropes) are classified as acidophilic cell types. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Besides acute stress responses, what other pattern controls baseline cortisol secretion?","choices":["Circadian rhythm","Seasonal variation only, with no daily pattern","Lunar cycle","Random, unpatterned fluctuation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cortisol secretion follows a circadian rhythm, in addition to being driven acutely by stress. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What relaxes the isthmus of the uterine tube, allowing the fertilized ovum to enter the uterus?","choices":["Inhibin from granulosa cells","Corpus luteum-derived progesterone","FSH from the anterior pituitary","Estrogen from the follicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Corpus luteum-derived progesterone relaxes the isthmus of the uterine tube, allowing entry of the ovum into the uterus. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Once filtrate leaves the collecting ducts as urine, what happens to its composition as it travels through the renal calyces, ureters, and into the bladder?","choices":["It is diluted substantially by ureteral secretion","It is extensively modified by tubular reabsorption","It is concentrated further by the ureter's countercurrent mechanism","It essentially remains unchanged"],"answer":3,"exp":"Fluid flowing out of the collecting ducts essentially remains unchanged in composition as it flows from the renal calyces through the ureters to the bladder. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Reduced perfusion pressure that triggers RAAS activation is detected by baroreceptors located in the:","choices":["Afferent arteriole","Efferent arteriole","Vasa recta","Distal convoluted tubule epithelium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Reduced perfusion pressure is detected by baroreceptors in the afferent arteriole, one of the three triggers for renin release alongside reduced Na+ delivery and sympathetic stimulation. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"The shallow resorption bay formed in bone directly beneath an active osteoclast is called:","choices":["A canaliculus","Howship's lacuna","The ruffled border","The epiphyseal plate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the resorption bay under an osteoclast is known as Howship's lacuna. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which of the three hormone classes is derived from cholesterol, is lipid soluble, and has very little storage?","choices":["Tyrosine-derived (amine) hormones","Eicosanoid hormones","Protein and peptide hormones","Steroid hormones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Steroid hormones are derived from cholesterol, are lipid soluble, and have very little storage (e.g., cortisol and aldosterone from the adrenal cortex, estrogen/progesterone from the ovaries, testosterone from the testes). — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Cortisol's increased blood glucose effect can lead to elevated insulin that becomes 'less effective,' a state sometimes described as:","choices":["Adrenal diabetes","Diabetes insipidus","Addison's crisis","Cushing reflex"],"answer":0,"exp":"This state — elevated insulin that is less effective due to cortisol's glucose-raising actions — is described in this lecture as 'adrenal diabetes.' — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Besides supporting the corpus luteum, hCG also inhibits uterine contractions caused by which hormone?","choices":["Relaxin","Prolactin","Cortisol","Oxytocin"],"answer":3,"exp":"hCG inhibits uterine contractions caused by oxytocin, helping maintain the pregnancy. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Net filtration pressure across the glomerular capillaries is the sum of four forces. Which force promotes filtration by pushing fluid out of the glomerular capillary?","choices":["Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG)","Hydrostatic pressure in Bowman's capsule (PB)","Colloid osmotic pressure of proteins in Bowman's capsule (πB), under normal conditions","Colloid osmotic pressure of glomerular capillary plasma proteins (πG)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Glomerular hydrostatic pressure (PG) promotes filtration by pushing fluid out of the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Before the countercurrent multiplier process begins (the theoretical starting point), the osmolality of the thin descending limb is:","choices":["Impossible to define","Lower than both the interstitium and ascending limb","Equal to the interstitium and thin ascending limb","Already maximally concentrated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Prior to multiplication, the theory assumes the osmolality of the thin descending limb is equal to that of the interstitium and thin ascending limb — the 'starting place' before the gradient develops. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Once the matrix calcifies around them, osteoblasts become which cell, residing within lacunae and canaliculi?","choices":["Osteoclasts","Osteocytes","Chondroblasts","Fibroblasts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: entombed osteoblasts become osteocytes within the canaliculi and lacunae. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which two hormones are grouped under the tyrosine (amine)-derived class alongside thyroid hormones?","choices":["Norepinephrine and epinephrine","Cortisol and aldosterone","Estrogen and progesterone","Insulin and glucagon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Norepinephrine and epinephrine (adrenal medulla catecholamines) are tyrosine-derived, grouped with thyroid hormones (T3/T4) in the amine class. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Where does glucagon act, per its basic hormone profile?","choices":["Skeletal muscle exclusively","Adipose tissue exclusively","The kidney","The liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glucagon acts primarily on the liver. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What is capacitation of spermatozoa?","choices":["Changes occurring after ejaculation that prepare sperm for fertilization","The maturation and storage process occurring within the epididymis","The process by which ejaculated semen coagulates and later liquefies","The initial formation of sperm in the seminiferous tubules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Capacitation refers to changes that occur after ejaculation — including loss of inhibitory factors, cholesterol loss weakening the membrane, and increased calcium permeability — that allow sperm to fertilize an ovum. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Summarized as a single pathway, the micturition reflex proceeds: Bladder Stretch → Pelvic Afferents (S2–S4) → Sacral Micturition Center → Parasympathetic Efferents → which final step?","choices":["Sympathetic efferent activation of the hypogastric nerve","External sphincter contraction only, with no detrusor involvement","Detrusor contraction and internal sphincter relaxation, leading to urination","Detrusor relaxation and internal sphincter contraction, preventing urination"],"answer":2,"exp":"The summarized pathway ends in detrusor contraction plus internal sphincter relaxation, producing urination. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What happens to the osmolality of the collecting duct fluid when ADH is absent (-ADH)?","choices":["It becomes iso-osmotic to plasma automatically","It increases substantially","It remains dilute","It matches the loop tip's ~1200 mOsm/kg exactly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Per the lecture's osmolality table, in the absence of ADH the collecting duct fluid remains dilute, since water cannot be reabsorbed there without ADH. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"During normal, non-forceful contractions, which motor units are recruited first?","choices":["Fast, white, glycolytic-fiber motor units","Slow-fiber motor units","Intermediate fibers exclusively","The largest-diameter fast fibers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: motor units containing slow fibers are recruited first for normal contractions. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 33"},{"q":"The 'Metabolic Actions of Growth Hormone Summary' groups GH's effects into which three categories, consistent with earlier slides?","choices":["Water, electrolyte, acid-base","Cardiac, renal, hepatic","Protein, fat, carbohydrate","Bone, muscle, skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"GH's metabolic actions are consistently summarized around protein synthesis, fat utilization, and carbohydrate utilization. — Endocrine Physiology I.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"During a chronic infusion of aldosterone, what ultimately happens to sodium excretion despite ongoing sodium reabsorption stimulation?","choices":["Sodium excretion falls to zero and remains there permanently","There is no describable long-term pattern in sodium excretion at all","Sodium excretion returns toward normal (aldosterone escape)","Sodium excretion increases without any limit as aldosterone continues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Despite aldosterone's ongoing stimulation of sodium reabsorption, chronic aldosterone infusion is associated with sodium excretion returning toward normal over time (aldosterone escape), rather than sodium retention continuing indefinitely. — 19. Endocrime Physiology II.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which gland secretes mucoid material containing fructose, citric acid, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen as part of the ejaculate?","choices":["Bulbourethral glands","Seminal vesicles","Epididymis","Prostate gland"],"answer":1,"exp":"The seminal vesicles secrete mucoid material containing fructose, citric acid, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen. — 20. Endocrine Physiology III-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"According to this lecture, which factor contributes to average GFR being about 10% lower in women than in men?","choices":["GFR does not actually differ by sex once body size is accounted for","Differences in sex hormone receptor density on podocytes","Women have a structurally different filtration barrier composition","Sex itself, alongside body size, is listed as a factor affecting GFR"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture lists sex — alongside body size — among the factors that affect GFR, noting that average GFR is about 10% lower in women than in men. — Renal I - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Aldosterone increases H+ secretion in which cell type of the collecting tubule/duct?","choices":["Intercalated cells","Principal cells","Mesangial cells","Renal podocyte cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Aldosterone increases H+ secretion specifically in intercalated cells, while increasing Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion in principal cells. — Renal II - Summer 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which feature allows smooth muscle to maintain force for hours, days, or weeks with minimal energy expenditure?","choices":["Continuous high-frequency firing of action potentials","The latch state of attached cross-bridges","Rapid cross-bridge cycling driven by high myosin ATPase activity","Ongoing release of acetylcholine at a motor end plate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the latch state lets smooth muscle maintain force for prolonged periods. — 23. Bone & Muscle Physiology, Slide 41"}]};
